    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Distance Between a Particle and the Origin",
    "problem": "A particle moves from the origin with an acceleration of $(-2i+j)\\,{\\text{ft/s}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $(4i+j)\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ Find the distance from the particle to the origin after $4$ seconds.",
    "worked_solution": "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=4i+j,\\,a=-2i+j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=4\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(0i+0j)+(4i+j)(4)+\\frac{1}{2}(-2i+j)(4)^{2} \\\\  & =(16i+4j)+8(-2i+j) \\\\  & =(16i+4j)+(-16i+8j) \\\\  & =12j\\,\\text{ft}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance from the particle to the origin at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| & =|12j| \\\\  & =|12| \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{ft}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
